preConditionHook:
// EL: hasBull = false, hasBear = false; for ii = 0 to withinBars - 1: sum
// high - low over the lookback bars ending at ii + 1, divide by lookback, and
// when high[ii] - low[ii] >= sizeMultiple * that mean, flag the bar as a large
// bull bar if close[ii] > open[ii] and a large bear bar if close[ii] < open[ii].
// Both flags are required, so the rule is direction-agnostic and the two sides
// carry the same text. A doji has no direction and counts for neither.
// 'Large' is measured against the mean bar range of the bars BEFORE the one
// being judged -- an exclusive benchmark, and high-low rather than an ATR, so
// gaps stay out of it and there is no second WFSafe_ call site alongside
// OppositeLargeBarPair. The EL twin writes the mean as an inner loop rather
// than Average(high - low, lookback)[ii + 1] because a function-history
// reference at a loop-variable offset is not a behaviour the register covers;
// Average recomputes fresh every bar over a price expression with real history,
// so the value is identical. Summed newest-first, EL's Summation order, and
// divided by the same (int) bound the loop uses.
// The scratch locals persist across bars, so all four are reset before use.
hasBull = false;
hasBear = false;
for (int i = 0; i < (int)withinBars; ++i) {
    rangeSum = 0.0;
    for (int j = 0; j < (int)lookback; ++j) {
        rangeSum = rangeSum + (high[i + 1 + j] - low[i + 1 + j]);
    }
    avgRange = rangeSum / (int)lookback;
    if (el_ge(high[i] - low[i], sizeMultiple * avgRange)) {
        if (el_gt(close[i], open[i])) {
            hasBull = true;
        }
        if (el_lt(close[i], open[i])) {
            hasBear = true;
        }
    }
}
